  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Cheyennes\"\nsubtitle: \"United States\"\nregion: \"North America\"\ntags: [ethnography, north-america]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Cheyenne, made up of the Tsétsėhéstȧhese and Só'taeo'o peoples. |\n| Where | The Great Plains, with Southern Cheyenne in western Oklahoma and Northern Cheyenne in southeastern Montana. |\n| How many | 12,130 combined people in 2008; about 10,840 Northern Cheyenne members in late 2014. |\n| Language | Cheyenne, an Algonquian language. |\n| Religion | Spiritual life centers on Ma'heo'o, the Sacred Arrows, the Sacred Buffalo Hat, sacred lodges, prophets, and the Sun Dance. |\n| Known for | Quillwork and beadwork · Painted buffalo-hide storage bags · Sacred Arrows · Sacred Buffalo Hat · Sun Dance |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Cheyenne are an Indigenous people of the Great Plains. The Tsétsėhéstȧhese and Só'taeo'o joined together in the early 19th century. Today they live mainly in Oklahoma and Montana. Their culture includes horse equipment, decorated clothing, sacred objects, and buffalo-hide bags.\n\n## Material culture\n\nCheyenne material culture is known for quillwork, beadwork, painted buffalo hide, clothing, horse equipment, and sacred bundles.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nCheyenne decoration uses porcupine quills, glass beads, paint, leather, and buffalo hide.\n\n- **Porcupine-quill decoration** (*quillwork*) — Quills were sorted, dyed, softened, flattened, and attached to clothing, moccasins, bags, and horse equipment.\n- **Bead decoration** (*beadwork*) — Glass beads were sewn onto leather tobacco bags, pipe bags, leggings, moccasins, shirts, and other clothing.\n- **Painted buffalo-hide bag** (*parfleche*) — A parfleche was a stiff untanned buffalo-hide bag for storing dried meat, sometimes painted red and green.\n- **Leather pipe bag** (*pipe bag*) — Leather pipe bags could have beadwork, glass beads, fringe, and quillwork.\n- **Saddle equipment** — A saddle with stirrups was recorded as Northern Cheyenne horse equipment.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nCheyenne clothing included decorated shirts, leggings, moccasins, blankets, jackets, and head ornaments.\n\n- **Decorated shirt** — Shirts could have quillwork, embroidery, beadwork, tassels, and fringe.\n- **Decorated leggings** — Leggings could be made from hide and decorated with beadwork or embroidery.\n- **Wool blanket** — Little Robe wore a wool blanket with quillwork around his waist.\n- **Warrior shirt** — High Toe wore a warrior shirt with quillwork leggings and moccasins.\n- **Military jacket** — Military jackets are included in the clothing records.\n\n### Architecture\n\nOlder Cheyenne settlements used earth lodges, but portable tipis replaced them after the move to nomadic horse culture.\n\n- **Earth lodge** — Earth lodges were formerly part of Cheyenne settlements.\n- **Tipi** — Portable tipis became part of Cheyenne life after the adoption of nomadic Plains horse culture.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nRecorded everyday objects include bags, pipes, rattles, saddles, and other horse equipment.\n\n- **Smoking pipe** — Museum records describe Cheyenne smoking pipes.\n- **Medicine rattle** — A medicine rattle appears in the museum records.\n- **Storage bag** — Storage bags were made from untanned buffalo hide and used for dried meat.\n- **Horse equipment** — Horse equipment included saddles with stirrups.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nCheyenne adornment included shell, metal, feather, and hair ornaments.\n\n- **Dentalium necklace or choker** (*dentalium*) — Records mention dentalium shell necklaces and chokers.\n- **Abalone ear ornament** (*abalone shell*) — Abalone shell ear ornaments appear in the catalogue records.\n- **Metal ear ornament** — Metal ear ornaments were worn with decorated clothing.\n- **Gorget** — Gorgets are listed among the recorded forms of adornment.\n- **Feather ornament** — Feather ornaments and eagle feathers were worn in the hair.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nThe sources focus on sacred objects and ceremonies rather than detailed music traditions.\n\n- **Sacred Arrows** (*Maahótse*) — The Sacred Arrows formed one of the two great covenants of the Cheyenne Nation.\n- **Sacred Buffalo Hat** (*Ésevone*) — The Sacred Buffalo Hat formed the other great covenant of the Cheyenne Nation.\n- **Ceremonial pipe** — A ceremonial pipe was associated with the Sacred Hat Bundle.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe Sun Dance is the only named Cheyenne dance, and the sources do not describe a theatre tradition.\n\n- **Sun Dance** — The Sun Dance was given to the people by Tomȯsévėséhe and was connected with the Sacred Buffalo Hat.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nCheyenne ritual life centers on the Sacred Arrows, the Sacred Buffalo Hat, their keepers, and sacred lodges.\n\n- **Sacred Arrows** (*Maahótse*) — The Sacred Arrows were received by Motsé'eóeve at Nóávóse, or Bear Butte, and kept in the maahéome.\n- **Arrow Lodge** (*maahéome*) — The maahéome was the Arrow Lodge or Arrow Tepee where the Sacred Arrows were kept.\n- **Sacred Buffalo Hat** (*Ésevone*) — The Sacred Buffalo Hat was received by Tomȯsévėséhe at Tȯhóonévose and symbolized female power.\n- **Sacred Hat Lodge** (*vonȧhéome*) — The vonȧhéome, also called hóhkėha'éome, was the Sacred Hat Lodge or Sacred Hat Tepee.\n- **Sacred Buffalo Hat Keeper** (*Tséá'enōvȧhtse*) — The Sacred Buffalo Hat Keeper had to belong to the Só'taeo'o.\n\n## Foodways\n\nCheyenne foodways changed from fishing and farming foods to a mainly bison-based Plains diet.\n\n- **Bison hunting** — The Cheyenne hunted bison and later relied mainly on bison after adopting Plains horse culture.\n- **Wild rice gathering** — They formerly collected wild rice while living between the Mississippi River and Mille Lacs Lake.\n- **Wild fruits and vegetables** — After moving onto the Plains, wild fruits and vegetables became part of their diet.\n- **Dried meat storage** — Painted buffalo-hide bags were used to store dried meat.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nCheyenne oral history tells how prophets received sacred objects and shaped Cheyenne society.\n\n- **Sacred Arrows story** (*Motsé'eóeve*) — Motsé'eóeve received the Sacred Arrows and organized military societies, legal justice, and the Council of Forty-four peace chiefs.\n- **Sacred Buffalo Hat story** (*Tomȯsévėséhe*) — Tomȯsévėséhe received the Sacred Buffalo Hat and gave the people ceremonies and the Sun Dance.\n- **Sweet Medicine prophecy** (*Sweet Medicine*) — Sweet Medicine predicted the coming of the horse, the cow, the white man, and other new things.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nCheyenne is an Algonquian language, and Cheyenne spiritual life centers on sacred objects, prophets, and ceremonies.\n\n- **Cheyenne language** (*Tsėhésenėstsestȯtse*) — Cheyenne is spoken in Montana and Oklahoma and has an alphabet of 14 letters.\n- **Só'taeo'o language** (*Só'taéka'ėškóne*) — The former Só'taeo'o language was so close to Cheyenne that it was sometimes called a Cheyenne dialect.\n- **Creator** (*Ma'heo'o*) — Ma'heo'o was the divine figure connected with the sacred objects received by the prophets.\n- **Sacred Hat Bundle** (*Hóhkėha'e*) — Hóhkėha'e was another name for the Sacred Buffalo Hat and its sacred tradition.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Tsétsėhéstȧhese* — One Cheyenne people\n- *Só'taeo'o* — One Cheyenne people\n- *quillwork* — Decoration made with porcupine quills\n- *beadwork* — Decoration made with glass beads\n- *parfleche* — Untanned buffalo-hide storage bag\n- *Maahótse* — Sacred Arrows\n- *maahéome* — Arrow Lodge or Arrow Tepee\n- *Ésevone* — Sacred Buffalo Hat\n- *Hóhkėha'e* — Another name for the Sacred Buffalo Hat\n- *vonȧhéome* — Sacred Hat Lodge or Sacred Hat Tepee\n- *hóhkėha'éome* — Sacred Hat Lodge or Sacred Hat Tepee\n- *Tséá'enōvȧhtse* — Sacred Buffalo Hat Keeper\n- *Nóávóse* — Bear Butte\n- *Tȯhóonévose* — Place where the Sacred Buffalo Hat was received\n- *Ma'heo'o* — Divine figure\n- *Tsėhésenėstsestȯtse* — Cheyenne language\n- *Só'taéka'ėškóne* — Former Só'taeo'o language\n- *dentalium* — Shell used for necklaces and chokers\n- *gorget* — Recorded neck ornament\n- *Sweet Medicine* — Prophet who predicted new things\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Wikipedia, “Cheyenne”: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheyenne\n- Museum of Ethnography, Museum of World Culture, Metropolitan Museum of Art, and British Museum catalogue records supplied with this atlas.\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription for this ethnic group is listed in the supplied sources.\n\n",
